    h1: "What Engineering Goes Into a Working Boat Repair Facility?",
    answer: "A boat repair yard is a working waterfront where vessels come out of the water — and the engineering has to handle that transition safely. The haul-out is the critical structure: a travel-lift pier designed for the lift's wheel loads at full vessel weight, or a marine railway with rails running down into the water. Upland, the work yard needs heavy pavement for boat stands and travel-lift traffic, washdown pads with full containment — bottom paint wash water is an environmental regulated waste — and shops for the marine trades: mechanical, electrical, fiberglass, and paint, each with ventilation and power to match. Stormwater from a boatyard carries metals and paint residue, so treatment is a permit requirement, not an option. I've seen yards try to operate washdown on open pavement; the environmental agencies always find them, and the retrofit costs far more than containment built in.",
    directAnswer: "A boat repair facility combines a haul-out structure — travel-lift pier or marine railway — with an upland work yard, washdown containment, and trade shops. Engineering covers the lift pier for full vessel weight, heavy yard pavements, wash water capture and treatment, stormwater permits, and shop ventilation and power for fiberglass, paint, and mechanical work.",
