    description: "Air cargo terminals need high-bay warehouse structures, aircraft aprons, specialized material-handling systems, and security zones meeting federal rules.",
    h1: "What Engineering Does an Air Cargo Terminal Truly Need?",
    answer: "An air cargo terminal is a warehouse with airplanes parked at the dock. The building itself is high-bay industrial — long clear spans, high ceilings for racking and material-handling equipment, and floor slabs engineered for forklifts and unit load devices. But the airside is what makes it specialized: aircraft apron pavement designed for the design aircraft's gear loads, with the drainage and lighting the airfield requires. Inside, the cargo operation needs screening areas meeting federal security requirements, refrigerated and specialized handling zones for perishables and pharmaceuticals, and truck docks on the landside sized for the cargo throughput. The terminal sits at the boundary of the airport's secure area, so access control and security design run through the whole project. I've seen cargo terminals designed as generic warehouses with the airside figured out later — the apron geometry and security boundary have to lead.",
    directAnswer: "An air cargo terminal combines a high-bay warehouse building with an aircraft apron and truck docks. Engineering covers long-span warehouse structures, floor slabs for material-handling loads, apron pavements for aircraft gear loads, federal security screening areas, cold-chain handling zones, and the landside truck circulation matched to cargo throughput.",
