    description: "Freight transfer terminals need dock-heavy buildings, trailer staging yards, and circulation geometry that keeps hundreds of daily trailers moving safely.",
    h1: "How Are Freight Transfer Terminals Engineered for Throughput?",
    answer: "A freight transfer terminal — the crossroads where long-haul trailers meet local delivery trucks — is engineered for vehicle throughput above all else. The building is a long, shallow dock structure: dozens or hundreds of dock doors on both sides, a narrow cross-dock floor between them, and a structural system — usually pre-engineered metal or tilt-up — that keeps the dock face open. The yard is the real engineering: trailer staging, tractor maneuvering, and circulation geometry for 53-foot trailers making constant moves, with pavement designed for the concentrated landing-gear and axle loads trailers impose when parked. Lighting has to cover the entire yard for 24-hour operation, and the gate and guard house control access. I've seen transfer terminals where the building was fine and the yard was a daily traffic jam — the site circulation is the project.",
    directAnswer: "A freight transfer terminal combines a shallow cross-dock building with a large trailer staging yard. Engineering covers the dock building structure, yard pavement for trailer loads, circulation geometry for 53-foot trailers, 24-hour yard lighting, stormwater for the paved footprint, and gate access control — all sized for daily trailer throughput.",
