    h1: "What Goes Into Engineering a Full-Service Trucking Terminal?",
    answer: "A trucking terminal is a truck driver's workplace and a fleet's operating base in one. The yard takes constant heavy-truck traffic — pavement engineered for fully loaded tractors, fuel islands with spill containment, and wash bays with water recycling and oil-water separation. The maintenance shop is a real industrial building: service bays with pits or lifts, compressed air, welding ventilation, parts storage, and waste-oil handling. Driver facilities — dispatch offices, rest areas, showers — need to be comfortable because drivers live out of these terminals. And the whole site needs security: fencing, lighting, and gate control, because a terminal full of cargo is a target. Environmental compliance threads through everything: fuel storage, wash water, waste oil, and stormwater all carry permits. I've seen terminals treat environmental as paperwork and learn the hard way that it's infrastructure.",
    directAnswer: "A trucking terminal pairs driver and dispatch facilities with a maintenance shop, fuel island, wash bay, and heavy-truck yard. Engineering covers industrial yard pavements, fuel spill containment, wash water treatment and recycling, shop ventilation and utilities, driver amenities, site security, and stormwater and environmental permits.",
