    h1: "How Are Airport Rideshare Lots Engineered for Dispatch?",
    answer: "Rideshare lots are a new building type born from the app era: a staging area where hundreds of drivers wait for trip requests, then flow to the terminal curb in dispatch order. The engineering is part parking lot, part logistics operation. The lot itself is large-scale pavement with lighting and striping for organized staging — often with numbered spaces tied to the dispatch system. Drivers wait in their cars for extended periods, so the lot needs restrooms, shade, and sometimes amenities; idling regulations in many jurisdictions push the design toward electrified waiting or shade structures with solar. The circulation to the terminal curb is the operational heart: a dedicated route that feeds the pickup zones in order without conflicting with taxis, buses, and private cars. I've seen airports try to handle rideshare on the existing curb; the dedicated lot exists because the curb can't absorb hundreds of staged vehicles.",
    directAnswer: "An airport rideshare lot is a staged waiting and dispatch facility for app-based drivers. Engineering covers large-scale staging pavement with dispatch-ordered layouts, driver restrooms and shade, circulation routes feeding the terminal curb, lighting and security for 24-hour use, and coordination with the airport's ground transportation plan.",
